#open-question(title: [Is One Well-Controlled Negative Decisive Against Several Poorly-Controlled Positives?])[
The best-designed study in this evidence base is negative, and the positive studies are weaker. Whether that settles the question depends on how much weight design quality can carry against a consistent direction of effect.

On the side of decisiveness: the null study was prospective, prespecified, excluded anticoagulant and antiplatelet users, used blinded confocal analysis, and analysed 151 participants @Fraser2026LCcoagulation. The positive reports were smaller, several scored particles visually without blinding, and at least one reported no quantified comparison against its control samples @Pretorius2022LCmicroclotPrevalence @Cochrane2023Plasmapheresis. On design alone the negative study outranks the set.

Three features keep the question open. First, *single timepoint*: one sample per participant at a single study visit cannot characterise a fluctuating state. Second, *subgroup size*: the fibrin and microaggregate analyses rested on 55 samples, which is adequate for a large effect and underpowered for a small one. Third, *pre-analytical handling*: fibrinogen is sensitive to freeze-thaw, and no fresh-versus-frozen comparison for microaggregates is reported, so a handling artefact upstream of the assay cannot be excluded.

One detail deserves care rather than rhetorical use. Fibrin fibre density was 47.9 $\pm$ 15.0 per 100~micrometres in controls, 43.6 $\pm$ 12.3 in breathless Long COVID, and 38.0 $\pm$ 11.0 in non-breathless Long COVID ($p = 0.22$, $n = 55$) @Fraser2026LCcoagulation. The direction runs *against* the positive literature. Dense networks of thin fibres are the prothrombotic pattern, and the patient groups had fewer fibres per unit length, not more; the authors read the result the same way, contrasting it with severe COVID-19, where density does rise @Fraser2026LCcoagulation. A mean roughly 20% lower in the non-breathless group is not a trivial difference, and it did not reach significance at this sample size, so the study did not resolve fibrin architecture in either direction. It weakens the denser-fibrin version of the hypothesis without excluding it.

*Falsifiable prediction:* a fresh-sample replication with power specified for a difference of at least 20% in fibre density would settle whether the difference is real and in which direction. A powered study showing equal density refutes the residual-abundance reading; one showing higher density in patients would support it and contradict this null.

*Consequence:* The correct statement is "no evidence of excess", not "evidence of no excess". For patients this distinction is not pedantic: a null result does not prove the mechanism absent, and it does not justify either starting or refusing a treatment on its own. (Origin: brainstorm.)
] <oq:null-decisiveness-and-design>
